<header class="farmers-v3-hero">
    <div class="farmers-v3-hero-main">
        <p class="farmers-v3-eyebrow">The Farmers · Evidence before portraiture</p>
        <h1>The work <em>before spice.</em></h1>
        <p class="farmers-v3-hero-dek">A useful account of large-cardamom farming begins with conditions, decisions and risks—not an invented face.</p>
    </div>
    <aside class="farmers-v3-standard" aria-label="Publication standard">
        <div>
            <span class="farmers-v3-ui">Publication standard</span>
            <strong>No profile without consent.<br>No quote without a source.<br>No portrait without permission.</strong>
        </div>
        <p>Real farmer profiles appear only after consented field interviews. Until then, this page documents verified sector context, practical decisions and open questions.</p>
    </aside>
</header>

<section class="farmers-v3-truth farmers-v3-section">
    <div class="farmers-v3-wrap">
        <div class="farmers-v3-split farmers-v3-reveal">
            <div>
                <p class="farmers-v3-eyebrow">01 · What can be said now</p>
                <h2>A crop tied to <em>livelihood.</em></h2>
            </div>
            <div class="farmers-v3-copy">
                <p class="farmers-v3-lead">Large cardamom is a high-value cash crop in Nepal’s hills and mountains, with production historically concentrated in the east.</p>
                <p>National and regional findings provide context. They do not stand in for direct Taplejung interviews or describe every household in the same way.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="farmers-v3-facts farmers-v3-reveal">
            <article>
                <b>99%</b>
                <h3>Export concentration</h3>
                <p>FAO’s 2025 market brief says almost all Nepalese large-cardamom exports went to India in 2023.</p>
                <a href="https://openknowledge.fao.org/handle/20.500.14283/cd6635en">FAO · 2025 market brief</a>
            </article>
            <article>
                <b>2022</b>
                <h3>National priority crop</h3>
                <p>Nepal selected large cardamom for FAO’s One Country One Priority Product initiative.</p>
                <a href="https://www.fao.org/one-country-one-priority-product/news-and-events/news/strengthening-the-value-chain-of-large-cardamom-in-nepal/en">FAO · OCOP update</a>
            </article>
            <article>
                <b>East</b>
                <h3>Historic production centre</h3>
                <p>ICIMOD identifies Taplejung, Panchthar, Ilam and Sankhuwasabha among major producing districts.</p>
                <a href="https://www.icimod.org/enhancing-the-large-cardamom-production">ICIMOD · regional programme</a>
            </article>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="farmers-v3-work farmers-v3-section">
    <div class="farmers-v3-wrap">
        <div class="farmers-v3-section-head farmers-v3-reveal">
            <div>
                <p class="farmers-v3-eyebrow">02 · The chain of decisions</p>
                <h2>What the farm must <em>manage.</em></h2>
            </div>
            <p>Farm practice varies by place, variety and household. These linked decisions show what later field interviews need to document carefully.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="farmers-v3-ledger farmers-v3-reveal">
            <article><span>i.</span><h3>Planting material</h3><p>Disease-free saplings and nursery access matter enough to be a current FAO programme priority.</p></article>
            <article><span>ii.</span><h3>Shade, soil and water</h3><p>ICIMOD frames soil condition and climate-resilient farm practices as part of sustaining production.</p></article>
            <article><span>iii.</span><h3>Harvest and movement</h3><p>Harvest timing, labour and movement of wet capsules set the conditions for post-harvest work.</p></article>
            <article><span>iv.</span><h3>Drying</h3><p>The farmer must convert a perishable harvest into a stable product while balancing fuel, time, capacity and quality.</p></article>
            <article><span>v.</span><h3>Sale</h3><p>Export concentration means local farm decisions meet a market system heavily oriented toward India.</p></article>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="farmers-v3-pressure farmers-v3-section">
    <div class="farmers-v3-wrap farmers-v3-pressure-grid">
        <div class="farmers-v3-reveal">
            <p class="farmers-v3-eyebrow">03 · Pressure on the farm</p>
            <h2>Value does not remove <em>risk.</em></h2>
            <p>These are sector-level issues from institutional and research sources. Their scale at a particular farm remains to be established through interviews and records.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="farmers-v3-pressure-cards farmers-v3-reveal">
            <article><b>01</b><h3>Pests and disease</h3><p>FAO identifies pest and disease control as a current value-chain priority; Nepal research repeatedly identifies chirke, foorkey and rhizome-related decline.</p></article>
            <article><b>02</b><h3>Planting material</h3><p>Tissue-culture laboratories and nurseries for disease-free saplings are part of Nepal’s OCOP support.</p></article>
            <article><b>03</b><h3>Post-harvest systems</h3><p>FAO’s 2025 market brief identifies post-harvest, certification and export infrastructure as areas being improved.</p></article>
            <article><b>04</b><h3>Market access</h3><p>Almost-total dependence on one export destination makes market access and diversification material questions for the chain.</p></article>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

